  "query_preview": "/Through Thursday/...\nIssued at 223 AM MDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nThere haven't been any notable changes to the forecast over the\nlast couple of days. I'm not sure if this pattern would \ntechnically qualify as an omega block - with two closed lows off \nthe Pacific Northwest and New England, with a large ridge in \nbetween - but either way most of Colorado will be \"blocked\" out of\nany meaningful pattern change over the next week or so. \n\nDry southwesterly flow aloft remains in place this morning, per\nwater vapor satellite imagery. The ridge centered across the south\ncentral CONUS is expected to gradually strengthen today and shift\na bit westward. We'll start to see a bit of moisture creep into\nthe southern half of our CWA by this evening, which could lead to\nisolated or widely scattered showers and a few thunderstorms late\nthis afternoon into the evening. Otherwise, temperatures should be\na couple degrees warmer than yesterday. \n\nThe flow aloft should turn to more of a S/SSW direction by this\nweekend, allowing for better moisture advection into the region.\nThis should result in widely scattered but relatively weak\nconvection across most of the high country. \n\nContinued moisture advection is likely for Sunday and Monday. \nWith an increase in boundary layer moisture, convection should be \nmore widespread but still limited in coverage given essentially no\nlarge scale/synoptic support.\n\nAs mentioned in the past few forecast cycles, models begin to\ndiverge by early or mid-week next week. However, the overall\npattern would still favor a ridge off to our east with some sort\nof cutoff low or open trough near the west coast. Moisture should\nremain over our area - leading to continued chances of afternoon\nshowers or thunderstorms - but significant/widespread rainfall\nstill appears unlikely with no notable shortwaves or fronts moving\nthrough.",
